module bindings.javascript.loop_restart_without_progress;

// A scanning loop can reach a restart -- a `continue` -- without passing any
// progress step for the variables its exit reads: the advance sits below
// the branch that restarts, so the exit re-evaluates over unchanged values,
// takes the same branch again, and the cycle cannot reach the exit (the
// loop spins for as long as the branch keeps matching, CWE-835). The
// classic spelling is a budgeted fetch loop, `while (next && i < limit) {
// if (skip(next)) continue; ...; next = body.next; i++ }`, where the skip
// branch freezes both the cursor and the counter.
//
// Both facts are structural and produced by the JavaScript frontend for
// this purpose, attributed to the innermost function that owns the loop.
// `loop_restart_without_progress` holds when some restart bound to the loop
// is dominated by no progress evidence -- no assignment, augmented
// assignment or update writing an exit variable, no method call receiving
// one as its receiver (the mutating idiom: `it.next()`, `queue.shift()`),
// and no call to a closure defined in the same function whose body carries
// such evidence (`getArg()` shifting the argument vector the condition
// reads) -- while the body carries evidence somewhere, so a loop whose exit
// is flipped elsewhere (`while (running)`) or reached only by `break` stays
// out. while, do-while and update-less `for(;;)` loops carry the fact: a
// for-loop with an update clause does not, because `continue` still runs
// it, nor do for-in/for-of loops, whose iterator advances mechanically; and
// a condition that assigns (`while ((line = read()) != null)`) advances
// every iteration. Nothing here names any API.
//
// `loop_progress_hidden_in_call` holds when the restart's dominating path
// carries a call to a bare name the function does not define -- an import,
// a parameter, a global. Such a call may advance the loop through effects
// the fact cannot see, which is a judgement for the rule, so it is emitted
// as a control and the rule's `unless` decides: a loop advanced only
// through an opaque call's side effects is not reported, and extending that
// decision is a rule edit, not a frontend one.
//
// Residual, stated: a restart whose branch self-clears the latch that
// selects it (`if (skip) { skip = false; continue }`) reports, because the
// cleared variable is not one the exit reads and the fact is per-restart,
// not carried across iterations; a labeled `continue` is skipped, since it
// binds to the loop its label names; and resolution looks one hop inside
// the analyzed function only, so a helper the file defines outside it is as
// opaque as an import -- the false-negative direction, neutralised in data
// by the control above.
binding loopRestartWithoutProgress {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and node.context.loopRestartWithoutProgress == "true"
  emit issue code.LoopRestartWithoutProgressCheck at node
}

binding loopProgressHiddenInCall {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function" and node.context.language == "javascript"
    and node.context.loopRestartWithoutProgress == "true"
    and node.context.loopProgressHiddenInCall == "true"
  emit check core.LoopProgressCallSideEffect at node {
    covers sameScope {
      anchor: node
    }
  }
}
